  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather will continue tonight through Tuesday.\n\nSurface high pressure will build south across the Great Lakes region \ntonight through Labor Day before drifting to the eastern seaboard by \nTuesday. This will maintain dry and tranquil weather across the \nregion through Tuesday. \n\nCool low level northerly flow may produce a few more lake effect \nclouds tonight through Monday morning over and south of Lake \nOntario. Otherwise, radiational cooling and light winds will allow \nsome fog to develop overnight through Monday morning in the valleys \nof the Southern Tier and Finger Lakes. There may be some patchy thin \nfog elsewhere, but the most prevalent fog will be across the \nSouthern Tier. Expect a similar distribution of fog again late \nMonday night through Tuesday morning.\n\nTemperatures will be quite cool tonight, dropping back into the \nlower 50s on the lake plains and mid 40s across the Southern Tier \nvalleys and North Country. A day to day warming trend will then \ndevelop Monday through the middle of the week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front will cross the eastern Great Lakes \nWednesday through Wednesday night, bringing the next chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms to the region.\n\nA sharp mid level trough will advance east across Ontario and Quebec \nWednesday through Wednesday night, with the southern extent of \nheight falls spreading east across the Great Lakes region. A narrow \naxis of greater moisture will be advected into the eastern Great \nLakes just ahead of the system, with surface dewpoints rising into \nthe mid to upper 60s by Wednesday afternoon and contributing to at \nleast some instability. A pre-frontal trough is forecast to advance \neast across the eastern Great Lakes Wednesday afternoon or Wednesday \nnight, with some timing differences still showing in latest model \nguidance. \n\nIncreasing moisture and forcing for ascent along and ahead of the \nmid level trough and pre-frontal trough will bring increasing \nchances of showers and thunderstorms later Wednesday through \nWednesday night. Shear will increase as a belt of enhanced mid level \nflow near the base of the trough moves into the area. There may be \nat least some limited severe potential if the timing of forcing \naligns with the diurnal heating cycle and sufficient instability \ndevelops.",
